      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "A four-digit integer must have the form ABCD, where A, B, C, and D are the digits to be used. Since we have the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9, we can calculate the number of different ways to arrange these digits using the concept of permutations.\n\nThe formula for permutations when there are repeated elements is given by:\n\n\\[ P(n, k) = \\frac{n!}{n_1! \\cdot n_2! \\cdots n_k!} \\]\n\nwhere \\( n \\) is the total number of items to arrange, and \\( n_1, n_2, \\ldots, n_k \\) are the frequencies of the distinct items.\n\nIn this case, \\( n = 4 \\) (the total number of digits), and there are two identical digits (2 and 9). Therefore, the number of distinct permutations of the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9 is:\n\n\\[ P(4, 4) = \\frac{4!}{2! \\cdot 2!} = \\frac{24}{4} = 6 \\]\n\nSo, there are 6 different positive four-digit integers that can be formed using the digits 2, 2, 9, and 9. The final answer is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{6}\n\\]",
